{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Not a dog shall whet his tongue.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): He likened the plague of the firstborn to a dog, to teach that at the time when harm is prevalent among men, the righteous are delivered from it, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 91:7): ״יפול מצדך אלף ורבבה מימינך״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: The significance of the dog's whetting in connection with the plague of the firstborn is well known — for the dog is a corporeal beast that is never sated, and so the prophet said (Isaiah 56:11): ״לא ידעו שבעה״, and the destructive force is never sated for all time, saying הב הב. And because carrion (נבלה) and torn flesh (טרפה) are stricken from the aspect of the destructive force, therefore the Torah commanded to cast the torn flesh and the carrion to the dogs, as it is said (Exodus 22:30): ״לכלב תשליכון אותו״ — because the dogs are from that same aspect. And this is what it says: ״ולכל בני ישראל לא יחרץ כלב לשונו״ — it teaches you that the dog is sharp of tongue, in the manner of what is written of the serpent (Ecclesiastes 10:11): ״ואין יתרון לבעל הלשון״. Now behold, the emanation of the destructive force comes to it from the power of the attribute of Judgment that smites all the firstborn of Egypt, and the attribute of Judgment is overwhelming at midnight; therefore it smote them and all their firstborn died. And Scripture reveals to you that at the midpoint of the second watch the dogs howled in Egypt, because the angel of death had come among them. And the Sages, of blessed memory, stated explicitly: dogs howl — the angel of death has come to the city; dogs laugh — Elijah has come to the city. And they further said in tractate Berakhot regarding the division of the watches of the night, which are three watches: in the first, a donkey brays — an allusion to the attribute of Ḥesed; and a donkey is not mentioned in the Torah until Abraham came. In the second watch, dogs howl — this is the attribute of Judgment; see how they placed in it an allusion to dogs howling. In the third watch, a woman converses with her husband, and an infant nurses from his mother's breasts — they placed in it an allusion to sustenance, for the Holy One, blessed be He, provides sustenance for every creature at that hour. And so Solomon alluded (Proverbs 31:15): ״ותקם בעוד לילה ותתן טרף לביתה וחק לנערותיה״ — the infant is all the world, and his mother's breasts are the woman conversing with her husband. If so, Scripture tells you the power of the great miracle: at that very hour when the dog was whetting his tongue against the Egyptians, he was not whetting it against all the children of Israel. And it added an explanation: ״למען תדעון אשר יפלה ה' בין מצרים ובין ישראל״ — that is, between Egypt, which is from the aspect of the dog, and Israel, who are from the aspect of the blessed Name, Who works wonders."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {1 "The phrase והנה אצילות הכח המשחית is rendered hyper-literally; אצילות here carries its kabbalistic sense of 'emanation' and is left to stand without further gloss per the voice rules."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:57:55.561463675Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}